Issue #539: A merge can close the kit containment graph into a cycle, and reading that kit then overflows the stack

The kit containment graph must be acyclic, and `addKitComponent` enforces that with a recursive descendant walk before it writes. That is a read-then-write check across sibling rows, so it holds on one device only: two devices that each make a locally-valid nesting move converge on a graph where kit X contains kit Y and Y contains X. The merge has a repair for exactly this shape on the two `parent_id` hierarchies, but not for the kit edge table — and the reader that walks the merged graph recurses without a cycle guard, so opening or assembling that kit overflows the stack in the database worker.

## What happens

`assertKitLinkValid` (`src/db/repositories/item/kits.ts:494-531`) walks the proposed component's descendant set and rejects a link that would close a loop:

```sql
WITH RECURSIVE descendants(id) AS (
  SELECT ? UNION
  SELECT kc.component_item_id FROM kit_components kc JOIN descendants d ON kc.kit_item_id = d.id )
SELECT id FROM descendants;
```

Device A adds kit Y as a component of X (legal — X is not below Y). Device B adds X as a component of Y (also legal on B). Each edge is a `kit_components` row with its own UUID and a *different* `(kit_item_id, component_item_id)` pair, so the `UNIQUE (kit_item_id, component_item_id)` index (`src/db/migrations/v1-initial.ts:1658`) does not fire, and the id-keyed union keeps both. The merged graph holds X→Y→X.

`reconcile` does run a cycle repair — but only over self-referencing `parent_id` columns (`src/features/sync/reconcile.ts:238-241`, `rejectParentCycles(local, localUpserts, 'locations')` and `… 'items'`). That helper reads `row.parent_id` (`reconcile.ts:1355`) and structurally cannot see a graph expressed as edge rows, so `kit_components` has no repair at all. The `items` case was given one under #190 with the reasoning that a persisted cycle "would make the next attach/detach on that chain hang the database worker" — the kit graph has the same exposure and no equivalent.

The consequence is worse than a wrong number. `readKitGraph` (`kits.ts:398-432`) discovers edges with a visited set and terminates, but then builds the tree with a plain recursion whose memo is written only *after* the children are built:

```ts
const build = (id: string): KitTreeNode => {
  const cached = built.get(id);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const m = meta.get(id)!;
  const components = (edgesById.get(id) ?? []) … .map((e) => ({ quantity: e.quantity, node: build(e.componentId) }));
  const node: KitTreeNode = { … };
  built.set(id, node);        // ← too late to break a cycle
  return node;
};
```

so `build(X)` → `build(Y)` → `build(X)` never hits the cache and recurses until the stack blows. The function's own comment says "Termination is guaranteed by the acyclic-containment invariant" — an invariant only a single device can uphold. Both public entry points go through it: `rollUpAvailability` (`kits.ts:155`), which the kit view calls to show how many are buildable, and `assemble` (`kits.ts:245`).

## Why it matters

After the merge, opening the kit crashes the worker rather than showing wrong data, and it does so every time — there is no way to see the offending link in the UI in order to remove it, because the screen that would show it is the one that crashes. Nested kits are a shipped feature (Kits v3 cascade assembly), so this is reachable by two people editing kit structure on two devices.

